## The Rail

### 1. Colors — design tokens only, never hardcoded

Define every color as CSS custom properties / Tailwind `@theme` tokens in **one place** (`src/index.css`), once.

Preferred token shape (HSL triplets for new work — shadcn/Tailwind-compatible; existing hex `@theme` values stay until migrated as a deliberate token change):

```css
:root {
  --background: 222 47% 6%;
  --foreground: 210 40% 98%;
  --primary: 239 84% 67%; /* brand seed */
  --primary-foreground: 0 0% 100%;
  --secondary: 217 33% 17%;
  --secondary-foreground: 210 40% 98%;
  --accent: 217 33% 20%;
  --muted: 217 33% 14%;
  --muted-foreground: 215 16% 65%;
  --border: 217 33% 22%;
  --radius: 0.5rem;
}
```

Rules:

- Components reference tokens (`bg-brand-600`, `bg-surface-card`, `text-slate-*` only if already tokenized / theme-mapped — prefer `brand-*` / `surface-*`). Never `#6366f1` or `bg-[#…]` in JSX.
- A genuinely new semantic color (success, warning, destructive) is added to `@theme` / `:root` first, then used.
- Border radius, shadow depth, and structural tokens are platform-wide — not re-decided per screen. Prefer existing scales (`rounded-xl`, `rounded-2xl`, `.premium-card`).

### 2. Turning ONE input into a full coordinated palette

Never ask for more than one or two inputs (primary brand color or a short vibe). Then derive:

1. Resolve input to a base hue (HSL from color, or map vibe → hue/saturation).
2. Clamp primary lightness into a readable range (~L 35–55% on light; adjust for dark surfaces so contrast holds).
3. Compute foreground automatically — near-white or near-black for WCAG AA (≥4.5:1). Never assume white/black without checking.
4. Derive secondary / muted / accent as fixed offsets of the **same hue** (vary S/L only).
5. Re-check contrast on the full set; iterate lightness until every pairing passes. Never ship a failing palette.

This is context hydration: fill unspecified design inside a constrained space — do not freehand per screen.

### 3. Components — established primitives only, never hand-rolled

Commit to existing ScanLogic primitives and patterns:

- Cards / panels: `premium-card`, `premium-card-gradient`
- Shell / nav: `AppShell`, `TabBar`, `ResponsiveNav`, `ResponsiveTabs`
- Feedback: `sonner` toasts; `PremiumModal` for premium gates
- AI bodies: `SafeMarkdown` / `LawyerMarkdown` — never unsanitized HTML
- Forms / lists: match neighboring module JSX (DocDraft, Tax Vault, Contracts, etc.)

Compose app-specific UI by wrapping these — never a parallel button/modal/card stack with duplicated internal styling.

Minimum **44×44px** touch targets (`--touch-min`, `.touch-target`). Verify custom size variants.

If introducing an external kit (e.g. shadcn later), adopt it project-wide for new primitives — do not mix one-off library forks.

### 4. Layout — named archetypes, not freehand invention

Select via intent / structure — do not “design a layout” from scratch:

| Archetype | Use when |
|-----------|----------|
| `suite-shell` | Suite tabs inside Dashboard + AppShell; bottom nav &lt; lg, sidebar ≥ lg |
| `module-home` | Module landing with header + scrollable content + primary CTA |
| `wizard-steps` | Multi-step flows (BizStart, registration, builders) |
| `ai-chat-immersive` | Multi-line AI answers — see `16-ai-mobile-chat.mdc` |
| `centered-form` | Auth, short settings forms, single-purpose capture |
| `settings-stack` | Vertical stacked rows / toggles (`SettingsPage`, module settings) |

Inventing a new archetype is a deliberate, flagged decision — not the default.

### 5. Typography & spacing — fixed scale, no arbitrary values

- Font sizes: Tailwind scale only (`text-sm` … `text-3xl`) — never `text-[17px]`.
- Spacing: default scale (4px multiples) — never `p-[13px]`.
- Fonts: keep the app’s curated stack from `index.css` / existing pages — no open-ended font picker.
- Inputs ≥16px on mobile (already enforced in `index.css`).

### 6. Self-healing build loop

After generating or editing UI:

1. Fix syntax / import errors.
2. Fix lint issues if configured.
3. Run `npm run build` and resolve failures.
4. Remove unused / dead styles and orphaned components.
5. If visual feedback is available (browser preview), verify the render — compile ≠ looks right.

A UI task is not done until the project builds successfully.

---

## Token enforcement

Never introduce in components: hardcoded colors, arbitrary spacing, arbitrary font sizes, arbitrary border radii, arbitrary shadows. New semantics → global token system first.

## Layout enforcement

Choose the closest existing archetype. New archetype only if none fit.

## Accessibility (required)

- WCAG AA contrast
- Keyboard navigation
- Visible focus states
- Accessible labels where appropriate
- ≥44×44px interactive targets

## Responsive design

Mobile-first on first implementation. Never defer responsiveness.

## Quality bar

Before completing UI work: remove duplicated patterns; simplify overbuilt layouts; consistent spacing, typography, tokens, and components.

Assume production SaaS quality (maintainable, reusable, accessible, consistent with this rail). If multiple valid implementations exist, pick the most maintainable and rail-aligned one.
